This may sound very strange coming from a professional Unix
programmer, and Unix expert, but if your goal is first and
foremost to learn C, then I'd suggest downloading the free,
Microsoft Visual Studios 2005 version of C++ (and the SDK
libraries, since they aren't bundled into the system as they are
under Unix).  It's a very restrictive environment, and I know of
no professional (Windows programmers included) who uses it, but
it does hide a lot of the underlying aspects from you.  Sooner
or later, of course, you'll have to address them, but there's no
use trying to learn everything at once.

One thing you might want to do fairly soon is to learn a good
editor.  The learning process can be painful, and is likely to
get in the way of learning C some of the times, but the editor
is the one tool you'll be constantly using, and it's worth
becoming proficient in a powerful editor early, because of the
time it will save you once you do learn it.

(My own environment under Windows is MSys, GNU make, gvim and
the Microsoft compiler, invoked from the command line.  But if
you're trying to learn C, you definitly don't want to take on my
makefiles or shell scripts right away.  GNU make is a complete
programming language in its own right.)

