It depends on whether the message comes from the compiler or the
linker.  You'll need both include files and libraries.

Normally, the install procedure should set things up so that the
compiler finds them by itself.  I've never installed straight
gcc under Windows, however, so maybe something is special about
it.

One thing that's worth paying attention to if you're installing
any Unix based tools.  Unix tools are generally very, very bad
about handling spaces in file names, and it's generally easier
if you arrange for the critical components to be in paths
without spaces.  So when the install tool suggests a path with
spaces (e.g. c:\Program Files\...), override it.

Also: Windows accepts both \ and / as directory separators.  A
lot of tools display it as \, most Unix based tools as /,
however, and many Unix based tools systematically consider \ as
an escape character.  So get used to using / when you see \.

Similarly, some of the "Unix-like" environments will insist on
using : as a path separator, which doesn't work under Windows
(where the convention is ;), and some of them (UWin and CygWin,
at least) remap the file system, so you have to constantly be
aware of which program is interpreting the file name you enter.
Unless you have a lot of Unix scripts which you absolutely need
to use, you're probably better off avoiding such systems until
you're far enough along to make the distinction instinctively.
(I use a Unix-like environment under Windows because my Windows
development work consists almost entirely of porting from Unix.
Porting applications which make extensive use of the Unix shell,
so I really, really need it.)

